  <section class="lp-section lp-paper" id="keep-or-replace"><div class="lp-wrap">
    <div class="lp-heading"><p class="lp-kicker">04 / Choose the right move</p><h2>Keep ZoomInfo,<br>buy less or replace it?</h2><p>A switch should solve a problem your team can name. Work out whether the issue is coverage, cost, unused features or the effort of using the system.</p></div>
    <div class="zi-decisions"><div><span>Keep it</span><h3>The useful parts earn their cost.</h3><p>Your buyers are covered, the data reaches the CRM correctly and the team uses the additional features. Check the renewal scope and price. A familiar working setup has value.</p></div><div><span>Reduce or supplement</span><h3>The gap is narrow.</h3><p>Some buyers lack numbers, or some licensed features go unused. Ask what a smaller renewal would cover. Compare targeted extra data with paying for a second broad platform.</p></div><div><span>Replace it</span><h3>A smaller purchase does the job.</h3><p>An alternative covers the essential accounts and workflow at an acceptable total cost. Plan the import, follow-up ownership and contract exit before moving the team.</p></div></div>
    <div class="zi-two-col zi-after"><div><h3>What are ZoomInfo users saying?</h3><p>G2 reviews include praise for company research, account insights and CRM workflows. Some reviewers also describe stale records, credit limits and complexity. Trustpilot includes buyer complaints about billing and cancellation, alongside positive experiences.</p><p>These are reported experiences, and the review sites include different audiences. Use them to decide what to investigate. They do not establish how the product will perform on your list.</p><p class="lp-sources"><a href="https://www.g2.com/products/gtm-workspace-powered-by-zoominfo/reviews" target="_blank" rel="noopener">G2 customer reviews ↗</a> · <a href="https://www.trustpilot.com/review/zoominfo.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Trustpilot reviews ↗</a></p></div><div><h3>What would we check in a real campaign?</h3><p>Start with suitable accounts and the person responsible for the buying decision. Keep mobile, direct and company numbers labelled separately. A number that rings without an answer is a different result from reaching the wrong person.</p><p>Check the list after import: ownership, stop requests, call notes and promised callbacks should remain usable. Our <a href="/resources/how-to-buy-cold-calling-leads/">cold calling lead-buying guide</a> helps you choose the next number. Use the <a href="/resources/best-crm-for-outbound-sales/">CRM comparison</a> and <a href="/resources/best-cold-calling-dialers/">dialler comparison</a> for tools the new data purchase leaves you needing.</p></div></div>
    <div class="zi-price-note"><h3>What about Sales Navigator and Clay?</h3><p><strong>LinkedIn Sales Navigator</strong> helps you research people and accounts. Check the contact data and calling route you would still need. <strong>Clay</strong> can organise enrichment across providers, with setup, data charges and workflow usage to account for. Either can support part of the process; compare the complete arrangement before replacing a subscription.</p><p class="lp-sources"><a href="https://business.linkedin.com/sales-solutions/sales-navigator" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sales Navigator's capabilities ↗</a> · <a href="https://www.clay.com/pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Clay's plans and usage model ↗</a></p></div>
  </div></section>

